**Invariant:** After round `r`, `dist[v]` is the minimum cost of an `s`-to-`v` walk using at most `r` edges in the retained graph, or `None` if no such walk exists. Copying the distances preserves shorter walks; reading only the previous round and appending one edge establishes the invariant. The `None` guard runs before addition, so unreachable distances never participate in arithmetic.

**Unboundedness:** The final scan tests the Nth relaxation round, after exactly `N - 1` completed rounds. A strict improvement requires a walk whose cost beats every walk with at most `N - 1` edges. Such a walk contains a negative cycle: otherwise removing its nonnegative cycles would produce a no-more-expensive simple path with at most `N - 1` edges. Its finite starting distance establishes reachability from `s`, and the filtering establishes reachability to `T`.

Conversely, if a reachable negative cycle remains, not all its edge inequalities can satisfy `dist[v] <= dist[u] + w`: summing those inequalities around the cycle would contradict its negative total weight. Therefore the final scan finds an improvement. Repeating that cycle before continuing to `T` yields arbitrarily negative costs. An unchanged earlier round is a fixed point, so early return cannot hide such a cycle.

**Boundary cases:** If `T` is unreachable, return `("unreachable", None)`. If `s == T`, the empty walk has cost zero; the result is zero unless a negative cycle is reachable from `s` and can return to `s`, in which case it is unbounded. Self-loops and parallel edges require no special handling.

**Worst-case bounds:** With `M` edges, time is `O(N(N + M))`, including the length-N array copies each round, and memory is `O(N + M)`. These deterministic bounds hold for every allowed input, including `M = 0`; they do not depend on expected queue behavior. Python integers avoid overflow, and distances considered use at most N edges, so their magnitudes are bounded by `N × 10^12`.

The violated invariant is that every thread in the block must reach the same block-wide barrier, unless the barrier’s conditional is uniform across the block. With `valid = 70`, threads 0–69 execute `__syncthreads()` while threads 70–95 skip it. This is invalid synchronization and can stall execution, but it does not guarantee that every device will hang. Successful immediate launch checks do not establish successful kernel completion.

Move the barrier outside the conditional. Every thread initializes its shared-memory slot, but only valid threads read the input.

```cpp
// barrier_fix.cu
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>

#define CHECK(call) do {                                      \
    cudaError_t err = (call);                                 \
    if (err != cudaSuccess) {                                \
        std::fprintf(stderr, "%s: %s\n", #call,               \
                     cudaGetErrorString(err));               \
        std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);                             \
    }                                                        \
} while (0)

__global__ void sum_valid(const float* input, float* output,
                          int valid) {
    __shared__ float values[96];
    const int t = static_cast<int>(threadIdx.x);

    // The conditional operator evaluates only its selected operand.
    values[t] = (t < valid) ? input[t] : 0.0f;
    __syncthreads();

    if (t == 0) {
        float sum = 0.0f;
        for (int i = 0; i < valid; ++i)
            sum += values[i];
        output[0] = sum;
    }
}

// input must hold at least valid floats; it may be null for valid == 0.
// output must hold one float. Buffers must remain valid until completion.
cudaError_t launch_sum(const float* input, float* output,
                       int valid, cudaStream_t stream) {
    if (valid < 0 || valid > 96 || output == nullptr ||
        (valid > 0 && input == nullptr))
        return cudaErrorInvalidValue;

    sum_valid<<<1, 96, 0, stream>>>(input, output, valid);
    return cudaGetLastError();
}

int main() {
    float host_input[96];
    for (float& x : host_input)
        x = 1.0f;

    float* input = nullptr;
    float* output = nullptr;
    cudaStream_t stream;
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreate(&stream));
    CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&input),
                     sizeof(host_input)));
    CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&output),
                     sizeof(float)));
    CHECK(cudaMemcpy(input, host_input, sizeof(host_input),
                     cudaMemcpyHostToDevice));

    // Exercise every supported valid count, including zero and 96.
    for (int valid = 0; valid <= 96; ++valid) {
        CHECK(launch_sum(valid == 0 ? nullptr : input,
                         output, valid, stream));
        CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(stream));
        float result;
        CHECK(cudaMemcpy(&result, output, sizeof(result),
                         cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost));
        if (result != static_cast<float>(valid)) {
            std::fprintf(stderr, "Failure: valid=%d, result=%g\n",
                         valid, static_cast<double>(result));
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }
    }

    CHECK(cudaFree(input));
    CHECK(cudaFree(output));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(stream));
    std::puts("All 97 cases passed.");
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

