For each slot, the ordering is producer → ready record → consumer → consumed record → next producer. The producer owns the slot until ready; the consumer then owns it until consumed; only afterward may the next producer overwrite it. Each wait is submitted before its corresponding event is recorded again, so event reuse preserves the intended generation. First use skips the consumed wait because no previous consumer exists. Every result has its own output location, and the loop uses only device-side stream waits—no device-wide synchronization or host synchronization between iterations.

Both transactions can commit at repeatable read: this is write skew. I’m interpreting “uncall” as “on-call.” PostgreSQL’s serializable isolation prevents this outcome, provided a serialization failure triggers a retry of the entire transaction.

Starting with Alice and Bob both on-call, this repeatable-read interleaving is possible:
1. Alice begins and counts two on-call doctors.
2. Bob begins and counts two on-call doctors.
3. Alice updates Alice’s row to off-call.
4. Bob updates Bob’s row to off-call.
5. Alice commits; Bob commits. Nobody remains on-call.

They update different rows, so there is no competing update to the same row for PostgreSQL’s repeatable-read conflict checks to reject. Each snapshot still shows the other doctor on-call; neither transaction protects the aggregate invariant.

Run this setup once:
```sql
CREATE TABLE doctors (
    name text PRIMARY KEY,
    on_call boolean NOT NULL
);
INSERT INTO doctors VALUES ('Alice', true), ('Bob', true);
```

Then run these transactions in two separate database sessions, concurrently.

Alice’s session:
```sql
BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
DO $$
BEGIN
    IF (SELECT count(*) FROM doctors WHERE on_call) > 1 THEN
        UPDATE doctors SET on_call = false WHERE name = 'Alice';
    END IF;
END
$$;
COMMIT;
```

Bob’s session:
```sql
BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
DO $$
BEGIN
    IF (SELECT count(*) FROM doctors WHERE on_call) > 1 THEN
        UPDATE doctors SET on_call = false WHERE name = 'Bob';
    END IF;
END
$$;
COMMIT;
```

On SQLSTATE `40001`, whether raised during a statement or at `COMMIT`, roll back if the transaction remains open, then retry from `BEGIN` in a new transaction: repeat the count, the decision, any resulting update, and the commit. Do not retry only the update or only the commit. The application must perform this retry loop; these SQL blocks are individual attempts.

Correctness follows from serializability: successful transactions have an equivalent serial ordering. Starting with two on-call doctors, the first transaction can leave one; the next then counts one and does not turn that doctor off. In the conflicting concurrent execution, PostgreSQL detects the read/write dependency cycle and prevents both attempts from committing. Thus, assuming all changes follow this protocol, every successful committed state retains at least one on-call doctor.

